1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
One counter, two threads, and each thread adds one. The answer should be two.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The statement is three steps: read the value into a register, add one, write it back.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Both threads read zero, both write one. One increment has disappeared.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Two legal outcomes, one and two. The counter is not wrong; the sequence is unprotected.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
A critical section is the part that touches shared data. Three promises are asked of it.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Strict alternation: one variable, turn, and each process waits for its own value.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Mutual exclusion holds: turn cannot be zero and one at the same time.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Turn one, process one in its remainder, process zero waiting: progress fails.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Two flags and one turn: each process raises its flag, then offers the turn to the other.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Both raise their flags; the second writer of turn loses the tie, and waits.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Thirty-four reachable states, counting the finished one, and none has both inside.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Progress holds and waiting is bounded: a process waits for at most one entry by the other.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A semaphore is an integer plus a queue, touched only by wait and signal.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Value five, three waits: two left, nobody blocked.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Waits minus signals cannot exceed the initial value plus the signals that ran.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Twenty waits, twelve signals: at most nineteen can pass from seven, so one blocks. Answer seven.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A bounded buffer needs two jobs done: keep the buffer private, and wait for space or an item.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Correct order: wait for a slot, then take the mutex, then insert, then release both.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Swapped: the consumer takes the mutex, finds no item, sleeps holding it.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The rule: never sleep while holding a lock another process needs to wake you.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A monitor is a module the compiler locks for you: one process inside at a time.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Waiting on a condition variable releases the monitor and puts you on that condition queue.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
After a signal the waiter must re-check its condition, because the state may have changed.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Semaphores can do everything monitors can; monitors make one class of mistake impossible.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Four conditions, all needed: exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Draw a resource allocation graph: processes as circles, resources as squares with dots.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Two processes, two files, each holding one and asking for the other: a cycle.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Break the cycle by ordering the resources, or by asking for everything at once.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Two instances of one resource, one of another: draw a cycle and no deadlock.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The free instance is the escape hatch: P2 completes, releases R2, P1 proceeds.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
With multiple instances, test for a completion order, not for a cycle.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Single instance: cycle means deadlock. Several instances: cycle is only a warning.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Banker: grant a request only if some completion order still exists afterwards.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
GATE 2018 Q39: available three of E, three of F, none of G.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
P0 finishes, then P2, then P1, then P3. The state is safe.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
One resource type: deadlock is impossible when n times (K minus one) plus one is at most m.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: say what is shared, and whether the statements are atomic.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: for a value question, enumerate the extreme interleavings, not the typical one.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: for a liveness question, name the property before you name the verdict.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Five traps: atomicity, the negative semaphore, sleeping under a lock, cycles, and safe against deadlocked.

